    <article>
      <p class="lead"><strong>A resistor color code is a standardized set of colored bands that encode a resistor's resistance and tolerance, defined by the international standard IEC 60062.</strong> Each color stands for a digit from 0 to 9, one band is a power-of-ten multiplier, and the last band is the manufacturing tolerance. Read brown-black-red-gold and you get 1, 0, times 100, plus or minus 5%, which is 1,000 ohms (1 k&Omega;) at &plusmn;5%. The Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/stem-suite/resistor-color-code-calculator/">Resistor Color Code Calculator</a> decodes 4, 5, and 6-band resistors, works backward from a value to the bands, and adds an Ohm's Law and LED resistor helper, all in your browser.</p>

      <p>This guide covers what each color means, how 4, 5, and 6-band resistors differ, how to tell which end to start reading from, and the properties the bands do not encode. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/">Calculators hub</a> STEM suite, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

      <div class="cav">Start here</div>
      <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Resistor Color Code Calculator?</h2>
      <p>The Best Answer Hub Resistor Color Code Calculator is a free, browser-based electronics tool that turns colored bands into a resistance value and back. It decodes 4, 5, and 6-band through-hole resistors plus 3-digit, 4-digit, and EIA-96 surface-mount codes, and it works in reverse to suggest the bands for a target value with the nearest standard E-series option. It also bundles an Ohm's Law solver and an LED resistor calculator with wattage warnings, and a colorblind-friendly text mode, with no signup and nothing uploaded.</p>
      <div class="sec">
