      </figure>
      <div class="callout"><div class="h">Gold and silver do double duty</div><p>Gold and silver appear in two places: as fractional multipliers (times 0.1 and times 0.01) and as tolerance colors (plus or minus 5% and plus or minus 10%). That is why band position, not just color, decides the meaning. The Best Answer Hub Resistor Color Code Calculator reads each band by its slot, so a gold multiplier is never mistaken for a gold tolerance.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Count the stripes</div>
      <h2 id="bands">4, 5, or 6 bands: what is the difference?</h2>
      <p>The band count tells you how many significant digits you have. A 4-band resistor uses two digits, a multiplier, and a tolerance. A 5-band resistor adds a third significant digit for tighter precision, common in 1% parts. A 6-band resistor adds a temperature coefficient band, in parts per million per kelvin, that says how much the resistance drifts with temperature (<a href="https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/tools/resistor-color-code-calculator/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">All About Circuits</a>).</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Bands</th><th>Structure</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="band">4-band</td><td>2 digits + multiplier + tolerance</td><td>General purpose, 5% or 10%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">5-band</td><td>3 digits + multiplier + tolerance</td><td>Precision, often 1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="band">6-band</td><td>5-band + temperature coefficient</td><td>Temperature-stable circuits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="cav">Orientation first</div>
      <h2 id="direction">Which end do you read from?</h2>
      <p>Start from the end away from the tolerance band. The tolerance band, usually gold or silver, is often wider and set slightly apart from the rest, so you turn the resistor to put it on the right and read left to right (<a href="https://www.electronics-tutorials.ws/resistor/res_2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Electronics Tutorials</a>; <a href="https://learn.sparkfun.com/tutorials/resistors/decoding-resistor-markings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SparkFun</a>). Reading a resistor backward is the single most common mistake, and because gold and silver never appear as first digits, a gold or silver band is your cue for which end is the tail.</p>
      <div class="codebox">
        <div class="dt">Decode, then reverse</div>
        <div class="codelines">Decode:  brown  black  red    gold
         1      0      &times;100   &plusmn;5%
         &rarr; 10 &times; 100 = 1,000 &#8486; (1 k&#8486;) &plusmn;5%

Reverse: 4.7 k&#8486;  &rarr;  yellow  violet  red
                     4       7      &times;100</div>
        <p class="codenote">Worked value per ComponentsInfo; reverse example per SparkFun.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Common misreads</div><p>Red, brown, and orange can look alike under poor light or on a dusty part, and a resistor read from the wrong end gives a completely different value. Good lighting, a magnifier, and a tool that shows the decoded value help. The Best Answer Hub Resistor Color Code Calculator highlights the reading direction and offers a labeled text mode for exactly these cases.</p></div>

      <div class="cav">Not on the label</div>
      <h2 id="power">What the color bands do not tell you</h2>
      <p>The bands encode resistance and tolerance, and nothing else. Crucially, they do not encode the power rating: how many watts a resistor can safely dissipate is set by its physical size, since a larger body sheds more heat (<a href="https://www.electronics-tutorials.ws/resistor/res_7.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Electronics Tutorials</a>). The value is also nominal within tolerance, so a 220-ohm resistor at 5% is in spec anywhere from 209 to 231 ohms, which is why a multimeter reading 215 is perfectly fine.</p>
